      "title": "Hypothetical Batching: Aggregate Throughput vs. Individual Request Performance",
      "body": "This post explores a hypothetical scenario regarding LLM batching. We're considering a situation where a batch size of 1 yields a total throughput of 22 tokens/second, while a batch size of 4 increases this aggregate rate to 55 tokens/second.\n\nIt's crucial to note that these figures represent total, aggregate throughput across all requests being processed. They do not reflect per-request latency, which was not measured in this hypothetical experiment. The implications for individual request response times are therefore unknown. Furthermore, these numbers are purely illustrative and not derived from actual tests.\n\nThis raises a question for discussion: under what conditions might prioritizing aggregate throughput (higher batch sizes) be acceptable, even if it potentially impacts individual request latency? What are the trade-offs for applications with different responsiveness requirements? We aim to understand the practical considerations beyond raw throughput numbers.",
