      "title": "Illustrative Batching Throughput: Aggregate Gains vs. Unmeasured Latency",
      "body": "This is a hypothetical exploration of batching's impact on LLM throughput. Imagine an experiment where increasing the batch size from 1 to 4 resulted in a rise in total output tokens per second from 22 to 55. These are aggregate figures, meaning they represent the combined processing power across all concurrent requests.\n\nIt's important to emphasize that these values are purely illustrative and not based on real-world measurements or benchmarks. A key limitation of this hypothetical scenario is that per-request latency was not assessed. Therefore, we cannot determine how increasing the batch size affects the response time for any single request.\n\nThe core question is how to interpret these aggregate gains. While higher batch sizes show a significant increase in total throughput, the lack of latency data means we don't know the cost in terms of individual request responsiveness. This is particularly relevant for interactive applications. What are the scenarios where prioritizing overall system throughput over individual request speed is a valid strategy?",
